Why Mortgage Rates Aren't One-Size-Fits-All
One of the biggest misconceptions homebuyers have is that mortgage rates are fixed and non-negotiable — that whatever number a lender quotes is simply what you get. In reality, mortgage rates are influenced by a combination of factors, and understanding them can put buyers in a much stronger position.

Rates are typically shaped by:
    • Credit score — higher scores generally unlock better rates 
    • Loan type — conventional, FHA, VA, and USDA loans all carry different rate structures 
    • Down payment size — a larger down payment can reduce your rate and monthly payment 
    • Debt-to-income ratio — lenders look at how much of your income is already committed to debt 
    • Loan term — 15-year loans typically carry lower rates than 30-year loans 
Daphne families who take the time to understand these factors before applying are often the ones who end up with noticeably better terms than those who simply accept the first offer they receive.

